Z||000000||fiero56||09-24-2001||08:18 PM||||I don't know anything about them. I'm currently building a 2.8 at the moment, so if it were a good idea, now would be the time. Its been &lt;60*F all day, and I'm freezing my a$$ off. <P>Are these things prone to fire, or any other damage? pros/cons? Possible? I don't like the idea of starting a fresh engine at &lt;30*F in the morning durring the winter.||||reg||5
Z||000001||Hank is Here||09-24-2001||09:10 PM||||I doubt that you need one in Southwest Ohio.  They are a pain in the butt (at least my opinion)  but are necessary in the colder regions, like Canada.  I am in PA and have never needed one for a gas engine.  On a IH 7.3 diesel once I had a freeze plug with a block heater in it blow out, I waited a few hours and fixed it along the road.  That was during the summer so there are issues all year round  <P>An aside when they were building the Alcan highway they never shut the vehicles off, just let them idle all night.  Also they changed the oil in the machines while they were still running, drain all the oil out then put in the plug and fill her up!  Since it was so cold the never got warm enough to cease ||||reg||1
